# The Folly of Taking Another's Property
Tamil title: பிறர்சொத்தைக் கவரும் பேதைமை

The river was in heavy spate and the flood overflowed its banks. Transport between this bank and the other was therefore interrupted. Even the boatmen could not operate their boats, so they tied them up on the bank and left. At that time Sambandar and his sacred company arrived there from Madurai.

Sambandar wished to go to the village of Kollampudur on the opposite bank and worship the Shiva there. Once the desire arose, he untied one of the boats secured at the bank, put his devotees aboard and boarded it himself. Standing in the boat, he sang the *Tevaram* beginning “Kottame Kamazhum.” The boat moved and reached the other bank. This is one of the incidents found in the life of Sambandar.

***

“Alas, my livelihood has been ruined!”

“Why are you crying like this? What happened?”

“What happened? Why ask me? Alas, what shall I do? How shall I live?”

“I ask you to tell me what happened, but you keep looking in every direction and sobbing as though you have lost something. What is this? I understand nothing!”

“What do you mean, ‘as though’? I truly have lost something. My property has been taken!”

“Property?”

“Yes, my property! I cannot find the very thing that fed a poor man like me. The river was heavily flooded and I could not cross. As usual, I tied the boat at its place and went home. I returned in less than two hours, and the boat was gone.”

“Who might have taken it?”

“We cannot even suppose that someone wanting to reach the other bank took it across because the boatman was absent. The flood has not fallen by the smallest degree. Even experienced boatmen like us cannot cross in this spate. How could an inexperienced person operate the boat?”

***

If a boat tied to the riverbank by its boatman disappeared, there can be no doubt that a conversation like this would have occurred. Whether it could really be true that Sambandar, through the flood of his divine grace, subdued the river's flood and piloted a boat that even its boatman could not operate is one question. But according to Sekkizhar's account, the proper course is to examine the event as narrated and identify what is true and false within it.

Can anyone call it honest for Sambandar to take and use a boat tied up by its owner without either informing him or obtaining his consent? Sambandar's Saivite religious zeal seems to have prevented him even from considering what should be done, what should not be done, and what no one does.

Or had Shiva, who previously sent Sambandar a pearl palanquin to ride, also sent the boat lying on the riverbank, saying, “Beloved one! We commend your devotion and understand your wish. Here is a boat. You and your army of devotees may board it, come to Kollampudur and worship me”? No. The story itself says the boat's owner had tied it to the bank.

When anyone takes property belonging to another without that person's knowledge, worldly law regards the object as stolen property and the taker as a thief, and imposes the appropriate punishment. This rule is observed not merely here but in every country that conducts society according to human reason. Yet the legend supplies no evidence that Sambandar was charged or punished for taking another person's property without his knowledge or consent.

Nor can we suppose that in Sambandar's time, some 1,300 years ago, anyone in this country was permitted to take and use another person's property however they wished without consent. Two thousand years ago, Tiruvalluvar laid down principles of worldly conduct not only for our land but for the whole world. The social order of our country after that time could not have been so degraded that no one questioned such conduct.

Could the age of Sekkizhar, who composed the *Periyapuranam*, have been such a barbarous age? That too is implausible. Sekkizhar is said to have lived about nine hundred years ago, when the cruelty of taking exclusive possession of another's property without consent could not have been accepted. Nevertheless, Sekkizhar says Sambandar did exactly that.

Some may ask, “It is improper to say that Sambandar deliberately seized another person's property. He and his company had to cross, so he took one of the boats lying on the bank. Is it right to call this a crime?” I have one thing to say to them. If these “intellectuals,” unwilling to admit that touching another's property without consent is wrong, wish to absolve the taker because of their affection for him, the legend still gives no evidence that Sambandar returned the boat to its former place after finishing his task. Nor does it say that he left someone at the landing to tell the owner, should he return, who had taken the boat and for what purpose. Sekkizhar says only that he took one of the boats lying on the bank.

Some devotees who seek refuge in Sambandar may next claim that he suddenly faced such an urgent need to cross that he had no time to make these arrangements. But if they reflect for a moment on what his emergency actually was, they will not make that claim.

What emergency confronted Sambandar? Standing on the riverbank, he saw the temple tower on the opposite side and consequently wished to go there. That alone created his desire to cross. Was this a task that had to be performed with extreme urgency? Did he act because some great danger on the opposite bank could not otherwise be prevented? Should these questions not at least be considered?

If Sambandar wished to worship the lord of Kollampudur, he could have done so while remaining on this bank. Standing at Rameswaram, he had worshipped Trincomalee and Tiruketheeswaram in Lanka, hundreds of miles away. Would any devotee believe that he could not worship Kollampudur from a distance of less than a quarter mile?

Thus the lesson this *Periyapuranam* episode teaches its readers is that attachment to a religion and to the god who leads it allows one to do anything in any manner and to take anyone's property without the owner's consent, because religious doctrine does not regard it as sin. I ask lovers of the puranas to consider whether this story can teach people any other lesson capable of producing good conduct or honesty.

9 March 1947
